<CaseStudy
  title="Swan's Market"
  subtitle="You can start this study trip on BART."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/10th%20Street%20Market%2C%20Oakland%2C%20CA%2C%20at%20sunset.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Swan's Market hero — courtyard in the market hall (photo needed)"
  heroCredit="Dreamyshade · CC BY-SA 4.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:10th_Street_Market,_Oakland,_CA,_at_sunset.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/village-homes/', label: 'Village Homes' }}
  next={{ href: '/vauban/', label: 'Vauban' }}
>
  <section class="case-reading mx-auto max-w-[820px] px-6 pt-12 sm:px-12">
    <p>Swan’s Market brings housing and neighborhood businesses into and around a historic Oakland market. The redevelopment opened in 2000, combining affordable rentals, cohousing and commercial space. EBALDC’s account describes a demanding effort to fit those uses into an existing piece of Old Oakland. <a class="source-link" href="https://ebaldc.org/swans-market-an-act-of-faith/">[Project source]</a></p>
    <p>This was not “one roof, one loan.” EBALDC describes distinct funding, design and construction arrangements, including new affordable housing alongside reuse of the market structure. That complexity is central to the lesson: a mixed community may need multiple coordinated ownership and financing pieces.</p>
    <h2>The neighborhood is already here.</h2>
    <p>Reuse begins with existing people, businesses and memories. The useful question is how new housing strengthens their ability to remain and participate. A friendly courtyard drawing does not settle displacement, commercial rents or whose needs define the project.</p>
    <p>Visit public businesses as a customer, not as if residents’ homes were an exhibit. If arranging a resident conversation, ask about maintenance, privacy and the relationship between the different parts of the development, as well as the lovely bits.</p>
    <aside class="research-note mt-8">Reading lens: project publications document the place and its intended design. They are not independent evaluations of affordability, energy performance or how every resident experiences life there. The questions here are our interpretation.</aside>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
<li>Study local nonprofit development capacity before assuming a European institution must be invented from scratch.</li>
<li>Map separate ownership, subsidies and operating responsibilities across mixed uses.</li>
<li>Assess reuse condition and tenant impacts before declaring a conversion faster or cheaper.</li>
</Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources"><a href="https://ebaldc.org/swans-market-an-act-of-faith/">EBALDC · the redevelopment story</a>. Reviewed October 2026. Approximate counts are identified; population changes over time.</Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
